export const MIS = [
  ['MAP_ELEMENTS', 'M_EM_SCALE_KEY', 'Mixes up the scale and the key (thinks the key shows how far apart places are).', 'Which part of a map shows how far apart two towns are on the ground?', 'The key', 'The scale', 'The child knows both are boxes in a corner and treats them as one thing.', 'Use a described map: the key lists symbols, the scale turns a ruler distance into ground distance. Ask for one job each.', 'The key tells what each symbol means. The scale tells how far a distance on the map is on the ground.'],
  ['MAP_ELEMENTS', 'M_EM_FRAME_TITLE', 'Mixes up the title and the frame (thinks the frame names the map or the title is the border).', 'Which element tells you what the map is about?', 'The frame', 'The title', 'Both sit at the edge of the map, so the child links them.', 'Cover the title and ask what the map is about; then ask what the border is for.', 'The title says what the map is about. The frame is the border line around the map.'],
  ['MAP_ELEMENTS', 'M_EM_COMPASS_ROLE', 'Thinks the compass on a map tells the time or the weather, or shows the symbols.', 'What does the compass drawn on a map show?', 'The symbols used on the map', 'Direction, such as north', 'The child has seen the compass as decoration only.', 'Ask the child to find north on the page before using any place name.', 'The compass shows direction. It tells you which way is north, south, east and west on the map.'],
  ['MAP_READING', 'M_EM_SYMBOL_GUESS', 'Guesses what a symbol means from how it looks instead of reading the key.', 'The key shows a small triangle for a school. A triangle also looks like a tent. What does the triangle on this map stand for?', 'A tent', 'A school', 'The child trusts how the symbol looks, not the key.', 'Change the key so a triangle means a church, then a school, and ask again each time.', 'Use the key. A symbol means what the key says it means, not what it looks like.'],
  ['MAP_READING', 'M_EM_SCALE_IGNORE', 'Reads map distance as ground distance without using the scale (1 cm taken as 1 km).', 'On a map, 3 cm stands for 30 km. How far apart are two towns that are 2 cm apart on this map?', '2 km', '20 km', 'The child reads the number on the ruler as the ground distance.', 'Make a scale line on paper and read it together before measuring anything.', 'Use the scale. If 3 cm stands for 30 km, then 1 cm stands for 10 km, so 2 cm stands for 20 km.'],
  ['NEIGHBOURS', 'M_LP_NEAR_NOT_BORDER', 'Names countries that are near Kenya but share no border (Rwanda, Burundi, Zambia, DR Congo).', 'Which country shares a border with Kenya?', 'Rwanda', 'Uganda', 'The child counts East Africa as one group and does not check borders.', 'Trace the border on a described map: which countries touch Kenya at a line? Rwanda lies behind Uganda and Tanzania.', 'Rwanda is in East Africa but does not touch Kenya. Kenya\'s five neighbours are Ethiopia, South Sudan, Uganda, Tanzania and Somalia.'],
  ['NEIGHBOURS', 'M_LP_OCEAN_AS_COUNTRY', 'Counts the Indian Ocean as one of Kenya\'s neighbouring countries, or forgets that it is water.', 'Which of these is a country that borders Kenya?', 'The Indian Ocean', 'Somalia', 'The child lists everything that touches Kenya on the map.', 'Ask: can a person have a passport from the ocean? Sort touching things into countries and water.', 'The Indian Ocean touches Kenya but it is water, not a country. Somalia is a country that shares a border with Kenya.'],
  ['NEIGHBOUR_DIRECTIONS', 'M_LP_EAST_WEST_SWAP', 'Swaps east and west when placing Uganda and Somalia.', 'In which direction from Kenya does Uganda lie?', 'East', 'West', 'The child remembers that Uganda and Somalia are on the sides but not which side.', 'Use the sea: the Indian Ocean is on the east side, so Somalia is east and Uganda is the other side.', 'Uganda is to the west of Kenya. Somalia and the Indian Ocean are to the east.'],
  ['NEIGHBOUR_DIRECTIONS', 'M_LP_NORTH_SOUTH_SWAP', 'Swaps north and south when placing Ethiopia and Tanzania.', 'Which country lies to the south of Kenya?', 'Ethiopia', 'Tanzania', 'The child links the "top" country to the south or confuses the order.', 'Use Mount Kilimanjaro: it looks over Amboseli, in the south, from Tanzania. Ethiopia is up toward the Horn of Africa.', 'Tanzania is to the south of Kenya. Ethiopia is to the north.'],
  ['FEATURE_NAMES', 'M_PF_KILIMANJARO', 'Thinks Mount Kilimanjaro is in Kenya, or is Kenya\'s highest mountain.', 'What is the highest mountain in Kenya?', 'Mount Kilimanjaro', 'Mount Kenya', 'Kilimanjaro is seen from Amboseli in Kenya and is famous, so the child places it in Kenya.', 'Show that Kilimanjaro stands on the Tanzanian side. Ask which mountain gave Kenya its name.', 'Mount Kilimanjaro is in Tanzania. Mount Kenya, at 5,199 metres, is the highest mountain in Kenya.'],
  ['FEATURE_NAMES', 'M_PF_TYPE_MIX', 'Mixes up the kind of feature (calls a lake a river or a mountain a plateau).', 'What kind of feature is Lake Turkana?', 'A river', 'A lake', 'The child knows the name but not the kind of water body.', 'Use the word in the name: lake or mount. Ask whether the water flows or stands.', 'Lake Turkana is a lake, a large body of water with land all round it. Rivers like the Tana flow in a line to the sea.'],
  ['FEATURE_WHERE', 'M_PF_REGION_MIX', 'Places a feature in the wrong part of Kenya (Lake Turkana in the south, Lake Victoria in the east).', 'In which part of Kenya is Lake Victoria?', 'The east', 'The west', 'The child remembers the feature but not its side of the country.', 'Link the feature to a neighbour: Lake Victoria is shared with Uganda and Tanzania, so it is on the west.', 'Lake Victoria lies in the west of Kenya and is shared with Uganda and Tanzania.'],
  ['FEATURE_WHERE', 'M_PF_RIFT_DIRECTION', 'Thinks the Great Rift Valley runs east to west, or along the coast.', 'In which direction does the Great Rift Valley run through Kenya?', 'East to west', 'North to south', 'The child has not seen a map of the valley and imagines a line across the country.', 'Describe the Rift lakes in order: Turkana in the north, then Baringo, Nakuru, Naivasha, Magadi toward the south.', 'The Great Rift Valley runs north to south through Kenya, past Lake Turkana, Lake Baringo, Lake Nakuru, Lake Naivasha and Lake Magadi.'],
  ['WEATHER_ELEMENTS', 'M_WC_WEATHER_CLIMATE', 'Treats weather and climate as the same thing (thinks climate is today\'s conditions).', 'Which statement is about climate and not weather?', 'It rained heavily this morning.', 'Mombasa is hot and humid for most of the year.', 'Both words are used for the air, and the child does not link climate to a long time.', 'Ask "how long did we watch?" One day is weather. Many years is climate.', 'Weather is what the air is like on one day. Climate is the usual pattern over many years.'],
  ['WEATHER_ELEMENTS', 'M_WC_INSTRUMENT_MIX', 'Matches a weather instrument to the wrong element (rain gauge for temperature).', 'Which instrument measures the amount of rain?', 'A thermometer', 'A rain gauge', 'The child knows the names but not the jobs.', 'Match each instrument to a unit: degrees, millimetres, direction.', 'A rain gauge measures rainfall. A thermometer measures temperature.'],
  ['CLIMATE_REGION_PLACES', 'M_WC_REGION_MIX', 'Swaps the hot dry north with the cool highlands when naming where a region lies.', 'Which part of Kenya has an arid climate, with very little rain?', 'The area around Mount Kenya', 'Turkana in the north-west', 'The child links "high" and "far" together, or links the big mountain with dry land.', 'Match three towns to rain: Nyeri (much rain), Mombasa (humid), Lodwar (very little).', 'The arid north, around Turkana and Marsabit, gets very little rain. The Mount Kenya area is cool and wet.'],
  ['CLIMATE_REGION_PLACES', 'M_WC_COAST_DRY', 'Thinks the coast is hot and dry like the north.', 'Which description fits Mombasa on the coast?', 'Hot and dry with very little rain', 'Hot and humid with plenty of rain', 'The child groups all hot places together.', 'Hot has two kinds: dry (Lodwar) and humid (Mombasa). Ask about the sea air.', 'The coast is hot and humid, with plenty of rain. Dry heat is the climate of the arid north.'],
  ['CLIMATE_REGION_TRAITS', 'M_WC_EQUATOR_HOT', 'Thinks everywhere near the equator is hot, so Mount Kenya has no cold.', 'Mount Kenya is on the equator. Why is it cold at the top?', 'It is not cold there.', 'The air is cooler as you go higher up.', 'The child connects the equator with heat and has not met height.', 'Ask: is it colder at the top of a high hill than at its foot? Then link to the mountain.', 'Even on the equator, the air gets cooler as you go higher. That is why Mount Kenya has snow at the top.'],
  ['CLIMATE_REGION_TRAITS', 'M_WC_HUMID_DRY', 'Thinks humid means dry, or that humid means cold.', 'What does it mean when the coast is humid?', 'The air is very dry.', 'The air has a lot of water vapour in it.', 'The child has heard humid together with hot and guesses.', 'Use clothes that do not dry on a humid day.', 'Humid means there is a lot of water vapour in the air, so the air feels damp and sticky.'],
  ['HISTORIC_SITES', 'M_BE_NATURAL_AS_BUILT', 'Counts a natural feature (Mount Kenya, a lake) as a historic built environment.', 'Which of these is a historic built environment?', 'Mount Kenya', 'Fort Jesus', 'The child links "famous place" with "historic site".', 'Sort cards: made by people, or made by nature?', 'Fort Jesus was built by people, so it is a built environment. Mount Kenya was not built by anyone.'],
  ['HISTORIC_SITES', 'M_BE_PLACE_MIX', 'Places a site in the wrong town (Fort Jesus in Lamu, Gedi in Nairobi).', 'In which town is Fort Jesus?', 'Lamu', 'Mombasa', 'Lamu and Mombasa are both old coastal towns.', 'Tell the stories: the Portuguese built Fort Jesus at Mombasa; Lamu Old Town is a town of coral stone houses.', 'Fort Jesus is in Mombasa. Lamu has its own old town, a historic town on an island.'],
  ['CONSERVATION', 'M_BE_KEEP_OUT', 'Thinks conserving a site means closing it to everyone or pulling it down to build new.', 'What is the best way to conserve an old fort?', 'Pull it down and build a new one', 'Repair it carefully and let visitors see it with rules', 'The child confuses old with useless or keeping safe with locking away.', 'Ask what a grandparent would lose if a home photo album were thrown away.', 'Conserving means looking after a place so it lasts. Careful repair and visitor rules keep it for later generations.'],
  ['CONSERVATION', 'M_BE_TOUCH_TAKE', 'Thinks that taking a small stone or writing a name on an old wall does no harm.', 'A visitor wants a small stone from an old wall. What should you say?', 'It is only a small stone, so it is fine.', 'No, if everyone took one the wall would be gone.', 'The child thinks one person cannot cause damage.', 'Ask what happens when 100 visitors each take one stone.', 'A small stone seems harmless, but many people doing it ruins the site. We leave heritage sites as we find them.'],
  ['GROUP_SORT', 'M_LG_NILOTE_AS_BANTU', 'Puts the Luo, Kalenjin or Maasai in the Bantu group.', 'To which language group do the Luo belong?', 'Bantu', 'Nilotic', 'The child knows the largest group is Bantu and sorts most communities into it.', 'Start with the Bantu list (Kikuyu, Luhya, Kamba), then name the Nilotes by their lake and plain homes.', 'The Luo are Nilotes. The Bantu group includes the Kikuyu, Luhya and Kamba.'],
  ['GROUP_SORT', 'M_LG_CUSHITE_AS_NILOTE', 'Puts the Somali, Borana or Rendille among the Nilotes (or among the Bantu).', 'To which language group do the Somali belong?', 'Nilotic', 'Cushitic', 'Many northern communities are herders, and the child groups herders together.', 'Group by language family, not by way of life: Maasai are herders and Nilotes, Somali are herders and Cushites.', 'The Somali are Cushites. Herding is a way of life; language group depends on the family of languages.'],
  ['INTERDEPENDENCE', 'M_LG_SELF_SUFFICIENT', 'Thinks each community can supply all it needs and has no need of others.', 'A fishing community has plenty of fish but no maize. What can it do?', 'Nothing, it must fish more', 'Trade fish for maize with a farming community', 'The child thinks of a community as separate from neighbours.', 'Ask each group what it has and what it lacks, then join the groups.', 'No community has everything. Communities swap goods and skills, so they depend on each other.'],
  ['INTERDEPENDENCE', 'M_LG_RIVALRY', 'Thinks depending on each other means competing or quarrelling.', 'What does interdependence between communities mean?', 'They compete to be the strongest', 'They help each other by sharing goods and skills', 'The child hears the word as a conflict.', 'Use a market scene where both sides gain.', 'Interdependence means helping each other. Both sides gain when they exchange goods, skills and ideas.'],
  ['DENSITY_AREAS', 'M_PD_DESERT_DENSE', 'Thinks the dry north is crowded because it is large, or that people prefer dry land.', 'Which area has a low population density?', 'The Mount Kenya highlands', 'The dry area around Marsabit', 'The child links size of an area with the number of people.', 'Ask what people need to farm and live: water and good soil. Then compare the two places.', 'Few people live in the dry north because there is little water and farming is hard. The highlands have rain and fertile soil.'],
  ['DENSITY_AREAS', 'M_PD_SIZE_DENSITY', 'Thinks a county with more people in total has the higher density, whatever its size.', 'Area A has 600 people on 3 square kilometres. Area B has 600 people on 6 square kilometres. Which is more crowded?', 'Both are the same', 'Area A', 'The child compares the number of people only.', 'Draw both areas on squared paper and place the 600 people in each.', 'Density is people per square kilometre. Area A has 200 people for each square kilometre and Area B has 100, so Area A is more crowded.'],
  ['DENSITY_EFFECTS', 'M_PD_DENSE_ALL_GOOD', 'Thinks a crowded area has only benefits (or no problems).', 'What is a problem of a very high population density?', 'There are no problems at all', 'Pressure on land, water and housing', 'The child links many people with many opportunities only.', 'List both: easy markets and many schools, but small plots and crowded roads.', 'High density brings pressure on land, water and housing, even though markets and services are close.'],
  ['DENSITY_EFFECTS', 'M_PD_SPARSE_NO_PROBLEM', 'Thinks a thinly settled area has no problems.', 'What is a problem of a very low population density?', 'Crowded schools and roads', 'Services such as schools and clinics are far apart', 'The child thinks empty means easy.', 'Ask how far a child walks to the nearest school in a thinly settled area.', 'Where few people live, services such as schools, clinics and roads are far apart and costly to provide.'],
  ['ATE_METHODS', 'M_TE_NO_SCHOOL', 'Thinks children had no education before schools and teachers came.', 'How did children learn before schools with classrooms were built?', 'They did not learn anything', 'From elders and parents through stories, work and songs', 'The child equates education with a classroom.', 'Ask how a child learned to herd or farm, and who taught her.', 'Children learned a lot before classrooms: elders and parents taught through stories, proverbs, songs and work.'],
  ['ATE_METHODS', 'M_TE_METHOD_MIX', 'Mixes up the methods (calls a proverb "role play", or learning by doing "storytelling").', 'A girl learns to cook by helping her aunt every day. Which method is this?', 'Storytelling', 'Learning by doing', 'The child knows the names but does not match them to a scene.', 'Describe three scenes and have the child name each: a story, a song, a task.', 'She learns by doing, working beside someone with the skill. Storytelling means learning from told stories.'],
  ['ATE_VALUES', 'M_TE_ONLY_SKILLS', 'Thinks traditional education taught only skills (herding, farming) and no values.', 'What did traditional education teach besides skills?', 'Nothing else', 'Values such as respect, honesty and responsibility', 'The child sees work but not the lessons inside the stories and customs.', 'Read a proverb and ask what it teaches about behaviour.', 'Traditional education taught values too: respect for elders, honesty, sharing and responsibility.'],
  ['ATE_VALUES', 'M_TE_OUTDATED', 'Thinks traditional education has no use today.', 'Why is African traditional education still useful?', 'It is not useful now', 'Its values and community lessons still guide behaviour', 'The child thinks old things are of no use.', 'Pick a value the school teaches and find the same one in a proverb.', 'Traditional education taught respect, honesty and teamwork. These values still help us live well with others.'],
  ['ADMIN_LEADERS', 'M_SA_DEPUTY_HEAD', 'Thinks the deputy headteacher is the head of the school.', 'Who is in charge of the whole school?', 'The deputy headteacher', 'The headteacher', 'The child confuses the one who acts in the head\'s absence with the head.', 'Ask who is in charge on a day when both are present and who helps.', 'The headteacher runs the whole school. The deputy helps the head and acts when the head is away.'],
  ['ADMIN_LEADERS', 'M_SA_DUTY_MIX', 'Gives a leader the duty of another (class teacher manages the school money, BOM teaches the class).', 'Who takes the class register each morning?', 'The Board of Management', 'The class teacher', 'The child knows the names but not who does what.', 'Match each duty to the person whose desk or room it is done at.', 'The class teacher looks after a class and takes the register. The Board of Management oversees the school as a whole.'],
  ['ADMIN_STRUCTURE', 'M_SA_ORDER_REVERSED', 'Puts the deputy headteacher above the headteacher on a chart.', 'On a school chart, who is placed directly under the headteacher?', 'The class teachers', 'The deputy headteacher', 'The child places the nearest staff at the top or mixes the order.', 'Draw the chart from the top down, one level at a time.', 'The deputy headteacher comes directly under the headteacher. Class teachers come below them.'],
  ['ADMIN_STRUCTURE', 'M_SA_PREFECT_ABOVE', 'Thinks prefects rank above teachers.', 'Who reports to the class teacher?', 'The headteacher', 'The learners and their class prefects', 'The child thinks prefects have power over staff.', 'Ask who gives prefects their duties.', 'Prefects are learners who help keep order. They report to teachers, who are above them in the school structure.'],
  ['RESOURCE_TYPES', 'M_RK_MONEY_ONLY', 'Thinks resources mean only money.', 'Which of these is a natural resource of Kenya?', 'Money in a bank', 'Water in Lake Victoria', 'The child thinks of wealth as cash.', 'Ask what people used before they had money.', 'Natural resources come from nature: land, water, forests, minerals and wildlife. Money is not a natural resource.'],
  ['RESOURCE_TYPES', 'M_RK_BUILT_NATURAL', 'Counts something people built (a road, a school) as a natural resource.', 'Which of these is a natural resource?', 'A school building', 'A forest', 'The child thinks that anything useful is a natural resource.', 'Sort cards into made by nature or made by people.', 'A forest grows by itself, so it is a natural resource. A school is built by people.'],
  ['PRUDENT_USE', 'M_RK_CONSERVE_NO_USE', 'Thinks conserving a resource means never using it.', 'What does it mean to use a resource prudently?', 'Never use it', 'Use it carefully so it lasts', 'The child thinks protection means keeping hands off.', 'Compare a family that uses water carefully with one that wastes it.', 'To use a resource prudently is to use it carefully so it does not run out and others can use it too.'],
  ['PRUDENT_USE', 'M_RK_UNLIMITED', 'Thinks resources such as forests and fish cannot run out because they grow back.', 'If people cut all the trees in a forest without planting new ones, what happens?', 'The forest grows back at once', 'The forest can disappear', 'The child sees "renewable" as "never ends".', 'Compare how long a tree takes to grow with how fast it can be cut.', 'Forests grow slowly. If we cut trees faster than they grow, the forest can finish. Replanting keeps it going.'],
  ['MINERAL_PLACES', 'M_MK_PLACE_MIX', 'Places a mineral in the wrong area (soda ash at Lake Nakuru, fluorspar at the coast).', 'Where in Kenya is soda ash mined?', 'The Kerio Valley', 'Lake Magadi', 'The child recalls the mineral and a Rift Valley place but mixes them.', 'Link each to a story: soda ash is taken from the lake water at Magadi; fluorspar is dug in the Kerio Valley.', 'Soda ash comes from Lake Magadi. Fluorspar is mined in the Kerio Valley.'],
  ['MINERAL_PLACES', 'M_MK_NOT_MINERAL', 'Counts a crop or timber as a mineral, or says Kenya mines diamonds.', 'Which of these is a mineral found in Kenya?', 'Coffee', 'Gold', 'The child treats any export as a mineral.', 'Ask whether it was dug from the ground or grown from a seed.', 'Minerals are found in the ground or rocks. Coffee grows on a plant. Gold is a mineral.'],
  ['MINERAL_IMPORTANCE', 'M_MK_USE_MIX', 'Gives a mineral the wrong use (limestone for jewellery, gemstones for cement).', 'What is limestone used to make?', 'Jewellery', 'Cement', 'The child knows the mineral names but not the products.', 'Trace the road from a mineral to a product people see: limestone to cement, gemstone to a ring.', 'Limestone is crushed and heated to make cement. Gemstones are cut for jewellery.'],
  ['MINERAL_IMPORTANCE', 'M_MK_NO_HARM', 'Thinks mining has no bad effects.', 'What harm can mining cause if it is not controlled?', 'None, mining is always good', 'It can damage land and endanger workers', 'The child sees only the money.', 'Describe an open pit left after mining and ask what it does to farmland.', 'Mining gives jobs and income, but if it is not controlled it can damage land, spread dust and put workers in danger.'],
  ['FISHING_GROUNDS', 'M_FK_SEA_AS_INLAND', 'Counts the Indian Ocean as an inland fishing ground.', 'Which of these is an inland fishing ground?', 'The Indian Ocean', 'Lake Victoria', 'The child thinks inland and fishing grounds are the same as any fishing place.', 'Inland means within the country, away from the sea. Sort water bodies on a described map.', 'Inland fishing grounds are lakes, rivers and dams inside the country. The Indian Ocean is the sea.'],
  ['FISHING_GROUNDS', 'M_FK_SODA_LAKE', 'Thinks Lake Magadi, a soda lake, is a main fishing ground.', 'Which lake is a main fishing ground?', 'Lake Magadi', 'Lake Turkana', 'The child sees every Rift Valley lake as a fishing lake.', 'Ask whether fish can live in water full of soda.', 'Lake Magadi has water too salty with soda for most fish. Lake Turkana and Lake Victoria are main fishing grounds.'],
  ['FISHING_GROUNDS', 'M_FK_REGION_MIX', 'Places a lake in the wrong part of the country (Lake Victoria in the east, Lake Turkana in the south).', 'Where is Lake Turkana?', 'In the south, near Tanzania', 'In the north-west of Kenya', 'The child remembers the name without a place.', 'Link the lake to the dry north and Lodwar town. Lake Victoria is western.', 'Lake Turkana is in the dry north-west of Kenya. Lake Victoria is in the west.'],
  ['FISHING_ECONOMY', 'M_FK_FOOD_ONLY', 'Thinks fishing only gives food to the fishing family.', 'How does fishing help the country as well as the family?', 'It only feeds the fishing family', 'It gives jobs and money from sales', 'The child sees fishing at home scale only.', 'Follow one fish from the boat to the market, the factory and the plate.', 'Fishing gives food, jobs for fishers, traders and processors, and money from selling fish at home and abroad.'],
  ['FISHING_ECONOMY', 'M_FK_NO_SUSTAIN', 'Thinks it is fine to catch all the fish, including the young ones.', 'Why should fishers not catch young fish?', 'There is no reason', 'So that fish can grow and breed for the future', 'The child sees only today\'s catch.', 'Compare catching all the chickens with keeping some to lay eggs.', 'Fish need to grow and breed. If we catch all young fish, there will be fewer fish later.'],
  ['ATTRACTIONS_PLACES', 'M_WT_PLACE_MIX', 'Places a park in the wrong part of Kenya (Amboseli in the north, Maasai Mara at the coast).', 'In which county is the Maasai Mara?', 'Kajiado', 'Narok', 'The child remembers southern Rift parks but mixes them.', 'Link them to a story: the Mara is where the wildebeest cross; Amboseli looks at Kilimanjaro.', 'The Maasai Mara is in Narok County. Amboseli is in Kajiado County.'],
  ['ATTRACTIONS_PLACES', 'M_WT_ANIMAL_MIX', 'Gives a park the wrong animal (flamingos at Amboseli, wildebeest at Lake Nakuru).', 'For which birds is Lake Nakuru National Park famous?', 'Ostriches', 'Flamingos', 'The child knows the park names but not what is special.', 'Tell the one thing each park is famous for.', 'Lake Nakuru is famous for flamingos, which gather at the shore, and for rhinos.'],
  ['TOURISM_ECONOMY', 'M_WT_HOTELS_ONLY', 'Thinks only hotel owners gain from tourism.', 'Who earns from tourism besides hotel owners?', 'No one else', 'Guides, drivers and craft sellers', 'The child sees the biggest businesses only.', 'List ten jobs on a day trip to a park.', 'Tourism supports many people: guides, drivers, cooks, craft sellers and local communities.'],
  ['TOURISM_ECONOMY', 'M_WT_ANIMALS_NO_VALUE', 'Thinks wildlife brings no benefit and the land would be better for farming.', 'Why does Kenya protect its wildlife?', 'Animals bring no benefit', 'Wildlife brings tourists and money to the country', 'The child sees the park as unused land.', 'Compare the income of a park visit with a small farm\'s harvest.', 'Wildlife attracts tourists, who bring money and jobs. Protecting it protects that income.'],
  ['EARLY_TRANSPORT', 'M_TR_EARLY_MODERN_MIX', 'Treats trains, lorries or aeroplanes as early means of transport.', 'Which was an early means of transport in Kenya?', 'A lorry', 'A donkey', 'The child does not tell old from new.', 'Sort cards by what the grandparents\' grandparents could have used.', 'Early means of transport include donkeys, canoes and people carrying loads. Lorries are modern.'],
  ['EARLY_TRANSPORT', 'M_TR_WALK_NOT_TRANSPORT', 'Thinks carrying loads on the head or back does not count as transport.', 'How did people move goods before roads and lorries?', 'They did not move goods', 'They carried loads on their heads and backs, or used animals', 'The child thinks transport needs a machine.', 'Ask how salt reached an inland home a hundred years ago.', 'Carrying loads on the head or back was an early means of transport, along with donkeys, camels and canoes.'],
  ['MODERN_TRANSPORT', 'M_TR_MODE_MIX', 'Chooses a means of transport that does not suit the job (a ferry to cross land, a lorry to cross a lake).', 'Which is the best way to carry many tonnes of fuel across the country?', 'A bicycle', 'A pipeline or a tanker lorry', 'The child links "carry" with any vehicle.', 'Compare tonnes and speed: a big load, a long way, or a small fast parcel.', 'Fuel in large amounts moves best by pipeline or tanker lorry. A bicycle carries a very small load.'],
  ['MODERN_TRANSPORT', 'M_TR_SGR_FIRST', 'Thinks the Standard Gauge Railway is the first railway in Kenya.', 'Which railway was built first in Kenya?', 'The Standard Gauge Railway', 'The railway from Mombasa to Kisumu', 'The child knows the new railway and not the old one.', 'Tell the story of the railway built from Mombasa toward Lake Victoria over 100 years ago.', 'The first railway ran from Mombasa to Kisumu and was built over 100 years ago. The Standard Gauge Railway is much newer.'],
  ['ROAD_SAFETY', 'M_TR_CROSS_ANYWHERE', 'Thinks it is safe to cross a road anywhere if it looks empty.', 'Where is the safest place to cross the road?', 'Anywhere, if the road looks empty', 'At a zebra crossing or footbridge', 'The child trusts what he sees at one moment.', 'Ask what a driver at speed can see around a bend.', 'Cross at a zebra crossing or footbridge. A road that looks empty can hide a fast vehicle.'],
  ['ROAD_SAFETY', 'M_TR_BELT_LATER', 'Thinks a seat belt or helmet is only needed on long trips.', 'When should a boda boda rider and passenger wear helmets?', 'Only on long trips', 'On every trip', 'The child thinks short trips are safe.', 'Ask how fast a crash can happen on a short road.', 'A crash can happen on any trip, short or long. Wear a helmet or seat belt every time.'],
  ['EARLY_COMMUNICATION', 'M_CM_EARLY_MODERN_MIX', 'Treats a telephone, radio or email as an early form of communication.', 'Which was an early form of communication in Kenya?', 'Email', 'A drum', 'The child cannot tell old from new.', 'Ask what was used before electricity.', 'Drums, horns and smoke signals were early forms. Email needs the internet and is modern.'],
  ['EARLY_COMMUNICATION', 'M_CM_EARLY_FAST', 'Thinks early forms reached as far and as fast as modern ones.', 'Why is a mobile phone faster than a messenger on foot?', 'It is not faster', 'It sends the message at once over a long distance', 'The child does not compare speed.', 'Time a runner and a phone call across the same distance.', 'A runner carries a message only as fast as he walks. A phone sends it at once, however far.'],
  ['MODERN_COMMUNICATION', 'M_CM_MEDIUM_MIX', 'Mixes up the means (says radio shows pictures, or a newspaper plays sound).', 'Which means of communication shows pictures and sound together?', 'Radio', 'Television', 'The child knows the names but not what each does.', 'Describe what you can see and hear on each in a one-line list.', 'Television shows pictures with sound. Radio has sound only.'],
  ['MODERN_COMMUNICATION', 'M_CM_NO_RISK', 'Thinks everything on the internet or a phone message is true and safe.', 'You get a message that claims something shocking. What should you do first?', 'Share it with everyone', 'Check if it is true with a trusted adult or source', 'The child trusts what he reads.', 'Practise with a made-up rumour and ask who could check it.', 'Not everything we read or are sent is true. Check first, then share.'],
  ['LEADER_FACTS', 'M_TL_COMMUNITY_MIX', 'Gives a leader the wrong community (Mekatilili as Maasai, Koitalel as Luo).', 'Mekatilili wa Menza led which community?', 'The Maasai', 'The Giriama', 'The child recalls a famous name without its community.', 'Link each leader with a map place: Mekatilili at the coast, Koitalel in the Nandi hills.', 'Mekatilili wa Menza led the Giriama of the Kenyan coast against forced labour.'],
  ['LEADER_FACTS', 'M_TL_ROLE_MIX', 'Mixes what each leader did (Nabongo Mumia led the Nandi resistance).', 'Which leader of the Nandi resisted the building of the railway?', 'Nabongo Mumia', 'Koitalel Arap Samoei', 'The child links all famous leaders to the same story.', 'Compare two leaders: one resisted, one welcomed visitors.', 'Koitalel Arap Samoei led the Nandi against the railway. Nabongo Mumia ruled the Wanga and welcomed the British.'],
  ['LEADER_QUALITIES', 'M_TL_FEAR_LEADER', 'Thinks a good leader is the one people fear.', 'Which quality makes a good traditional leader?', 'Making people afraid', 'Wisdom and fairness', 'The child links power with fear.', 'Ask whom a villager would go to for help and why.', 'Good leaders are respected for qualities such as bravery, wisdom and fairness, not because they are feared.'],
  ['LEADER_QUALITIES', 'M_TL_NO_ROLE_TODAY', 'Thinks traditional leaders and elders have no role today.', 'What do elders still do in many communities today?', 'Nothing, they have no role', 'Settle disputes and guide ceremonies', 'The child thinks old leaders are only a thing of the past.', 'Ask who settles a quarrel between neighbours first.', 'Elders still settle disputes, guide ceremonies and teach values in many communities.'],
  ['EARLY_FORMS', 'M_EG_NO_GOVT', 'Thinks Kenyan communities had no government before colonial rule.', 'How were communities governed before colonial rule?', 'They had no government', 'By councils of elders and other leaders', 'The child thinks government means a modern state.', 'Ask who settled disputes and made rules in a village before colonial rule.', 'Communities had their own governments: councils of elders, age-sets, spiritual leaders and, in some places, kings.'],
  ['EARLY_FORMS', 'M_EG_KING_ALL', 'Thinks every community was ruled by a king.', 'Which of these communities was ruled by a king?', 'The Maasai', 'The Wanga', 'The child knows kings from stories and applies them to all.', 'Compare the Wanga kingdom with the council-ruled Ameru.', 'Most Kenyan communities were ruled by councils of elders. The Wanga kingdom, under the Nabongo, was one with a king.'],
  ['MAASAI_AMERU', 'M_EG_SAME_NAMES', 'Mixes the titles (Njuri Ncheke as Maasai, Laibon as Ameru).', 'What was the council of elders of the Ameru called?', 'Laibon', 'Njuri Ncheke', 'The child knows the names but not which belongs to which.', 'Link the Maasai laibon to the spiritual leader and the Njuri Ncheke to the Ameru elders.', 'The Njuri Ncheke is the council of elders of the Ameru. The laibon was a Maasai spiritual leader.'],
  ['MAASAI_AMERU', 'M_EG_ONE_RULER', 'Thinks the laibon or the Mugwe ruled alone as a king.', 'How was the Maasai laibon different from a king?', 'He ruled alone as a king', 'He guided spiritually while elders made decisions', 'The child thinks a leader with a title has total power.', 'Ask who made decisions about cattle raids and grazing.', 'The laibon gave spiritual guidance. Decisions were made by elders and age-sets, so he was not a king.'],
  ['LESSONS', 'M_EG_OLD_USELESS', 'Thinks nothing from early government applies to today.', 'Which practice from early government is still useful today?', 'None of them', 'Settling quarrels by talk and mediation', 'The child sees early government as outdated.', 'Match each old practice to a modern office.', 'Settling quarrels by talk, consulting wise people and serving the community are still good practices.'],
  ['LESSONS', 'M_EG_COPY_ALL', 'Thinks modern government should copy everything from early government, including harsh punishments.', 'Which old practice should NOT be copied today?', 'Consulting elders', 'Punishments that hurt people', 'The child sees all old practices as good.', 'Sort practices into keep and leave behind with reasons.', 'We keep good practices such as consulting elders. Harsh punishments break rights and are left behind.'],
  ['HOW_CITIZEN', 'M_CZ_BORN_IN_KENYA_ONLY', 'Thinks a person must be born inside Kenya to be a citizen by birth.', 'Aisha was born abroad. Her mother is a Kenyan citizen. Is Aisha a Kenyan citizen by birth?', 'No, she was not born in Kenya', 'Yes, because one parent is a Kenyan citizen', 'The child links citizenship with place of birth only.', 'Use two stories: one born in Kenya, one born abroad to a Kenyan parent.', 'A child is a Kenyan citizen by birth if at least one parent is a Kenyan citizen, even when born abroad.'],
  ['HOW_CITIZEN', 'M_CZ_ID_MAKES', 'Thinks getting an ID card or passport is what makes a person a citizen.', 'What makes a person a citizen?', 'Having an ID card', 'Being born to a citizen or registered as one', 'The child confuses the paper with the status.', 'Ask: is a baby a citizen? Does she have an ID?', 'An ID card is a document that proves citizenship. A person is a citizen by birth or registration.'],
  ['HOW_CITIZEN', 'M_CZ_MARRIAGE_INSTANT', 'Thinks marrying a Kenyan makes a person a citizen on the wedding day.', 'A foreign woman marries a Kenyan man. When can she apply for citizenship by registration?', 'The same day', 'After a period set by law, seven years', 'The child skips the waiting period.', 'Tell the story with the years in order.', 'Marriage to a Kenyan does not give citizenship at once. A person may apply to be registered after seven years of marriage.'],
  ['DUAL', 'M_CZ_ONE_PASSPORT', 'Thinks Kenya does not allow a person to hold two nationalities.', 'Can a Kenyan citizen also be a citizen of another country?', 'No, Kenya does not allow it', 'Yes, the 2010 Constitution allows dual citizenship', 'The child recalls older rules from older people.', 'Ask: what year is the Constitution?', 'The 2010 Constitution allows a Kenyan to hold the citizenship of another country too.'],
  ['DUAL', 'M_CZ_DUAL_LOSE', 'Thinks a citizen by birth loses Kenyan citizenship by taking another nationality.', 'A Kenyan by birth takes the citizenship of another country. What happens to her Kenyan citizenship?', 'She loses it', 'She keeps it', 'The child thinks one nationality cancels the other.', 'Use a two-flag picture: two homes, one family.', 'A Kenyan citizen by birth does not lose Kenyan citizenship by getting another nationality.'],
  ['GOOD_CITIZEN', 'M_CZ_ONLY_VOTING', 'Thinks a good citizen is only someone who votes.', 'Which action shows good citizenship for a child?', 'Waiting until age 18 to do anything', 'Keeping the school compound clean and obeying rules', 'The child thinks citizenship begins at voting age.', 'List things a ten-year-old can do for the school and the village.', 'Good citizens obey rules, respect others and care for the environment at any age, not only when they vote.'],
  ['GOOD_CITIZEN', 'M_CZ_RIGHTS_NO_DUTY', 'Thinks citizens have rights but no duties.', 'Which is a duty of a citizen?', 'To do whatever one likes', 'To obey the law', 'The child hears the word rights more than duties.', 'Pair every right with a duty in a two-column list.', 'Citizens have rights and duties. One duty is to obey the law.'],
  ['ARMS_DUTIES', 'M_NG_ARM_MIX', 'Gives an arm the job of another (courts make laws; Parliament settles disputes).', 'Which arm of government makes laws?', 'The Judiciary', 'The Legislature', 'The child knows three names but not which job goes with which.', 'Use one verb per arm: runs, makes, explains.', 'The Legislature, or Parliament, makes laws. The Judiciary explains the law and settles disputes in courts.'],
  ['ARMS_DUTIES', 'M_NG_PRESIDENT_ALL', 'Thinks the President alone makes laws, runs courts and the government.', 'Who makes laws for Kenya?', 'The President alone', 'Parliament', 'The child thinks one person is in charge of everything.', 'Show that each arm checks the others.', 'Laws are made by Parliament. The President leads the Executive that carries them out.'],
  ['ARMS_MEMBERS', 'M_NG_MEMBER_MIX', 'Puts a person in the wrong arm (a judge in the Executive, a minister in the Judiciary).', 'To which arm do judges belong?', 'The Executive', 'The Judiciary', 'The child knows the people but not their arm.', 'Place each title in one of three boxes by its job.', 'Judges and magistrates belong to the Judiciary. The President and Cabinet belong to the Executive.'],
  ['ARMS_MEMBERS', 'M_NG_COUNTY_NATIONAL', 'Thinks governors and MCAs are part of the three arms of the National Government.', 'Which of these is part of the National Government\'s Executive?', 'A county governor', 'The Deputy President', 'The child merges county and national levels.', 'Draw two levels: county and national.', 'A county governor leads a county government. The Deputy President is part of the national Executive.'],
  ['SYMBOLS', 'M_NU_COLOUR_MEANING', 'Gives the flag colours the wrong meaning (black for the land, green for the people).', 'What does the red on the Kenyan flag stand for?', 'The people of Kenya', 'The blood shed in the struggle for freedom', 'The child remembers the colours but not the order of meanings.', 'Use a sentence: black people, red blood, green land, white peace.', 'Red on the flag stands for the blood shed in the struggle for freedom. Black stands for the people.'],
  ['SYMBOLS', 'M_NU_SYMBOL_MIX', 'Names something that is not a national symbol as one (Mount Kenya, a football team).', 'Which of these is a national symbol of Kenya?', 'The Safari Rally', 'The national anthem', 'The child thinks anything famous is a symbol.', 'List the official symbols and ask if the rally is among them.', 'The national flag, the coat of arms and the national anthem are official symbols. The Safari Rally is a sports event.'],
  ['UNITY_FACTORS', 'M_NU_UNITY_SAME', 'Thinks unity means everyone must be the same or speak one language.', 'What does national unity mean?', 'Everyone is the same', 'People of different communities live together in peace', 'The child hears unity as uniformity.', 'Ask whether a football team where all are the same does well.', 'Unity does not mean being the same. It means people of different communities live and work together.'],
  ['UNITY_FACTORS', 'M_NU_TRIBE_FIX', 'Thinks tribalism is solved by ignoring it or by separating communities.', 'How can we overcome tribalism?', 'Keep communities apart', 'Mix, share and respect all communities', 'The child thinks separation prevents quarrels.', 'Describe a school with mixed friends and a school with split groups.', 'Tribalism is overcome by mixing, sharing and respecting each other, not by keeping apart.'],
  ['BASIC_RIGHTS', 'M_HR_WANT_AS_RIGHT', 'Calls a want (a phone, sweets) a right.', 'Which is a human right?', 'A new phone', 'Education', 'The child thinks anything wanted is a right.', 'Sort cards into needs that protect life and dignity and things that are nice to have.', 'Rights protect life and dignity, such as education and health. A new phone is something we want.'],
  ['BASIC_RIGHTS', 'M_HR_ADULT_ONLY', 'Thinks only adults have rights.', 'Who has the right to education?', 'Only adults', 'Every child', 'The child sees rights as a grown-up matter.', 'Read the children\'s rights list aloud.', 'Every person has rights, including children. Children have the right to education.'],
  ['RIGHTS_RESPONSIBILITY', 'M_HR_NO_LIMIT', 'Thinks a right allows someone to do anything, even if it harms another.', 'Eve says she has a right to play loud music at night. What is missing?', 'Nothing, she may do it', 'Respecting the right of others to rest', 'The child sees rights only for himself.', 'Ask whose rights are touched when the music is loud.', 'Our rights end where they harm another person. Each right comes with a duty to respect it in others.'],
  ['RIGHTS_RESPONSIBILITY', 'M_HR_NO_REPORT', 'Thinks a child who is abused should keep quiet because it is a family matter.', 'A child is beaten badly at home. What should another child do?', 'Keep quiet because it is a family matter', 'Tell a trusted adult or call Childline 116', 'The child fears trouble for the family or for himself.', 'Practise telling a teacher and call Childline 116.', 'Abuse is never a secret to keep. Tell a trusted adult, a teacher or Childline 116.'],
  ['TYPES', 'M_DM_TYPE_MIX', 'Swaps direct and representative democracy.', 'The class votes by raising hands to choose a game. What type of democracy is this?', 'Representative democracy', 'Direct democracy', 'The child knows the names but not the meaning.', 'Ask: did each person decide, or did chosen people decide for them?', 'When every person votes on the matter, that is direct democracy. In representative democracy we elect others to decide.'],
  ['TYPES', 'M_DM_NOT_DEMOCRACY', 'Counts rule by one person as a type of democracy.', 'Which one is a democracy?', 'A leader who rules for life without elections', 'Voters elect their leaders', 'The child thinks any government is a democracy.', 'Ask who chose the leader and who can remove him.', 'In a democracy, people choose their leaders. A leader who keeps power without elections is not a democratic leader.'],
  ['BENEFITS_PRACTICE', 'M_DM_MAJORITY_ALL', 'Thinks that after a vote the minority no longer matters.', 'After the vote, 12 learners chose football and 8 chose netball. What is fair?', 'The 8 learners are ignored', 'Football is played, and the 8 are still respected', 'The child thinks winning means the others lose everything.', 'Ask how the 8 can still get a turn another day.', 'The majority decides, but the minority is still respected and can speak and try again next time.'],
  ['BENEFITS_PRACTICE', 'M_DM_LOUDEST', 'Thinks the loudest candidate or the one the teacher likes should win.', 'What is the fair way to choose a class monitor?', 'The loudest learner is chosen', 'Learners vote in secret and the votes are counted', 'The child sees choice as who is strongest.', 'Run a mock election with secret ballots.', 'A fair election uses a free, secret vote, counted honestly. Shouting does not decide it.'],
];
